# Arena Player — Admin. Copy to .env.local and fill. Never commit .env.local.
# Seven vars. Four are shared infrastructure with arena-player-web; three are this app's own.
# Setup notes live in docs/database.md (Supabase Postgres, Supabase Storage) and
# docs/architecture.md (auth, cron).

# --- Shared infrastructure ---------------------------------------------------

# Supabase TRANSACTION POOLER connection string — port 6543, host ends
# ".pooler.supabase.com". The direct connection exhausts connections under
# concurrent serverless invocations, and the expiry cron and a page load can
# overlap. The pooler is also why the client sets `prepare: false`: pgbouncer in
# transaction mode hands a different backend connection to each statement, so a
# prepared statement created on one is not there for the next.
#
# SAME SUPABASE PROJECT AS arena-player-web. Two projects produce an admin app
# that confirms bookings the public site never sees — both apps work, nothing
# errors, the whole product is a no-op. This app writes bookings.status; it
# never runs DDL.
DATABASE_URL=

# Supabase project URL, e.g. https://<ref>.supabase.co. Same project as web.
SUPABASE_URL=

# The proofs bucket. PRIVATE — no public URL is ever created, by either repo.
# Same bucket as web writes into: a different one means every proof_key resolves
# to a missing object and renders as the expired-link recovery, so the admin
# sees a recovery button that never recovers anything.
SUPABASE_PROOFS_BUCKET=arena-player-proofs

# The ANON key, never the service_role key. The bucket is private and an RLS
# `select` policy on storage.objects scoped to it is what lets this key read —
# which keeps the credential read-only by construction. Three reasons, any one
# sufficient: an admin-side compromise then cannot delete or overwrite payment
# evidence; web's credential is handed to the client at handover, so sharing one
# key means one rotation breaks two apps; and the admin has no legitimate reason
# to ever write to the bucket. service_role is full-access and discards all
# three silently.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --- This app's own ----------------------------------------------------------

# BOTH OF THE NEXT TWO ARE OPTIONAL UNDER `pnpm dev`, AND REQUIRED FOR
# `pnpm build` / `pnpm start`. Under `next dev` the login route accepts any
# password and the session is signed with a throwaway development key, so the
# dashboard is reachable with this whole section empty. Both bypasses are gated
# on `NODE_ENV === "development"` and are eliminated from a production bundle —
# see DEV_LOGIN_BYPASS in src/app/api/auth/login/route.ts and getSecret() in
# src/server/auth/session.ts. A HALF-FILLED VALUE IS WORSE THAN AN EMPTY ONE:
# a mangled hash rejects every password and reads as "wrong password".

# argon2id hash of the admin password. THE HASH, NEVER THE PASSWORD.
# Generate with `node scripts/hash-password.mjs` (hash-wasm, not @node-rs/argon2 —
# Sumopod's build environment is unverified and native bindings fail at deploy time).
# Starts with $argon2id$.
#
# EVERY `$` MUST BE ESCAPED AS `\$` IN THIS FILE. Next.js's built-in .env loader
# expands `$name` in a value by looking up another env var called `name` — an
# unescaped argon2id hash (all `$`-delimited fields) gets silently mangled into
# a short garbage string that never matches any password, which looks exactly
# like "wrong password" with no error anywhere. The hash-generation script
# prints the correctly-escaped line ready to paste. See docs/architecture.md,
# "Hashing".
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=

# HS256 signing key for the session JWT. >= 32 random bytes, base64.
# Rotating it logs the admin out; that is the intended behaviour, not a bug.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
SESSION_SECRET=

# Bearer token the external scheduler sends to POST /api/jobs/expire.
# Without a scheduler holding this value, pending bookings are never expired and
# abandoned slots on the PUBLIC site stay held forever. See docs/PRD.md Phase 3.
CRON_SECRET=
